course: dictionary
lesson: 11274
title: Wring: squeeze, worry and great effort
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Wring is a verb, so it is something you do. Its forms are wring, wrings, wrung and wringing. Read the words around it to find its meaning. «Cleaning machines do the boring jobs people once did by hand: scrubbing clothes, wringing them out, beating carpets and pressing creases.» The clothes are wet and they are being squeezed, so wringing them out means twisting them hard to get the water out.
explain: Wring can also describe worry. «Sam stood at the door, wringing his hands.» Nobody is washing their hands here. If someone is rubbing and twisting their hands, they are probably worried. Ask yourself: why would a person do that? The clues point to worry.
explain: Wring can mean getting something with great effort, as in «The teacher had to wring the truth out of the nervous boy.» The boy did not want to tell, so the teacher had to work hard to get the truth. Try your meaning in the sentence: does "get with great effort" still make sense? Then it works.
fact: «Wring» means to twist and squeeze something hard to get water out of it.
fact: «Wring your hands» means to rub and twist your hands together because you are worried.
fact: «Wringing wet» means very wet, as in a swimming costume that has been in the sea.
fact: «Wring something out of someone» means to get it with great effort.
fact: The forms of the verb are wring, wrings, wrung and wringing, so you might write «She wrung out the mop» for something that happened in the past.
exercise: «Cleaning machines do the boring jobs people once did by hand: scrubbing clothes, wringing them out, beating carpets and pressing creases.» What does wringing them out mean? | *Twisting and squeezing them to get the water out | Washing them in the sun | Beating them flat | Folding them neatly
exercise: «Sam stood at the door, wringing his hands.» What does this show? | He is very happy | *He is worried | He is clapping for joy | He is very cold
exercise: Jo came out of the sea, «wringing wet». What does wringing wet mean? | Slightly damp | Wet with paint | Dry and warm | *Very wet
test: «Mum wrung out the swimming costume before hanging it up.» What did Mum do? | Put it in the washing machine | Ironed it flat | Painted it bright blue | *Twisted and squeezed the water out of it
test: «The teacher had to wring the truth out of the nervous boy.» What does wring mean here? | Make him laugh | Forget about him | *Get it with great effort | Shout at him until he cried
exam: Which phrase means that a person is worried? | *Wringing their hands | Washing their hands | Clapping their hands | Holding their hands
